    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:57:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Public Hospitals in Iran: Organization, Financing and Reform",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s public hospitals, their links to medical universities, and the distinction between hospital reform objectives and documented results."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "institutional-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hospitals within a public service network",
        "content": "Iran’s public hospitals operate within a system that links health administration with medical education. The Ministry of Health and Medical Education, commonly abbreviated MoHME, operates hospitals through universities of medical sciences. Other institutions, including the Social Security Organization, also maintain hospital networks. Public provision therefore comprises different institutional arrangements rather than a single hospital operator. [s1]\n\nThis distinction matters when interpreting a hospital’s identity. University affiliation describes an administrative and educational relationship; public insurance describes a mechanism for paying for care. These are related features of the system, but they answer different questions about a facility. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "financing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Budgets, insurance and affordability",
        "content": "The Commonwealth Fund’s country profile describes hospital financing through budget allocations and payments from health insurers. Public hospitals generally offer more affordable care than private hospitals, although public ownership does not establish that every service is free. Insurance arrangements and patient payments remain part of the financing picture. [s1]\n\nThe World Health Organization’s assessment places hospital financing within a broader reform agenda: extending insurance, reducing patient cost-sharing, changing benefit packages and improving how services are purchased. These are policy objectives, and their inclusion in a reform programme does not by itself demonstrate that households are adequately protected from medical costs. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "management-reforms",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What hospital autonomy means",
        "content": "Hospital autonomy means granting managers greater discretion while potentially retaining public ownership. A research review published in July 2021 examined Iranian initiatives involving autonomous hospitals, boards of trustees, independent hospitals and corporatized facilities. These labels described attempts to change governance and incentives; they did not necessarily signify privatization. [s3]\n\nThe review found a recurring mismatch between responsibility for performance and control over resources. Hospitals could face pressure to earn revenue while retaining restricted authority over staffing, assets or strategic decisions. Its synthesis identified unstable financing, payment mechanisms, governance weaknesses and limited engagement with stakeholders as barriers to reform. [s3]\n\nThe authors also emphasized gaps in evaluation. Evidence from selected facilities and short study periods could not establish how every public hospital performed. Consequently, a hospital’s formal governance label should not be treated as proof of managerial independence, efficiency or service quality. The practical questions are what decisions managers can make, how services are financed and how performance is assessed. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
